l sample sizes (typically \\(n < 30\\)), the sampling distribution of the sample mean should be approximately normally distributed. This assumption can be relaxed for larger sample sizes due to the Central Limit Theorem, which states that the distribution of the sample mean approaches normality as the sample size increases.\n3. **Independence**: The observations in the sample must be independent of one another.\n\nThe violation of these assumptions can lead to misleading confidence intervals. For example, if the sample is not random, the interval may not accurately reflect the population parameter. Similarly, if the normality assumption is violated in small samples, the confidence interval may be too narrow or too wide, leading to incorrect inferences.\n\nIn summary, confidence intervals are a crucial tool in statistical estimation, providing a range of plausible values for population parameters while quantifying the uncertainty associated with sample estimates. Their proper application hinges on adherence to the underlying assumptions, which, if violated, can compromise the validity of the conclusions drawn from the data. The Gujarati textbook emphasizes the significance of understanding these intervals not only for their mathematical formulation but also for their practical implications in econometric analysis and decision-making.", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": [], "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "protected_material_code": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "protected_material_text": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}], "created": 1771166156, "model": "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": "fp_f97eff32c5", "usage":